//   person's actions. Hours ledger carried from chapter 8 (43.0 total, 1.6 solo on Nov 6) and chapter 10 (3.6 night, 10 night full stops);
//   flights not timed in earlier chapters are given plausible times here and summed in the 61.109 prop. With G.pushedweather the chapter 10
//   Dec 13 dual night flight (off 5:12, landed 5:51, after civil twilight ended about 5:06) adds 0.7 total, dual, and night and 1 night full stop. Shifts continue every 3 days from
//   chapter 10: Dec 14, 17, 20, 23, 26, 29, Jan 1, 4, 7, 10, 13 (7 AM to 7 AM). No frequencies appear on purpose.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 11, part: `Part Two: The Trip`, title: `The Written`, weeks: `December to January`,
  tasks: [`I.A`,`I.B`,`XII`,`III.A`,`I.H`,`I.E`],
  opening: [
    `Wednesday, December 16. Sunset at 4:37, civil twilight over at 5:07, and at 5:12 Biscuit started rolling, which Abby had timed down to the minute so the whole flight would go in the night column with nobody arguing about it.`,
    `Runup on Alpha near the end of 35 Right. Mags, carb heat, both door latches, and the top one on her side twice. She told ground she was ready for departure, and ground told her to monitor tower. Abby had both hands around a thermos, and as far as Jordan could tell she didn't touch the controls once in two hours.`,
    `It was a half moon, a little less, high in the south and bright enough to put shadows under the wing struts on the ramp. South of the shelf Abby had her climb to nine thousand five hundred, and the I-25 headlights ran out ahead of them over Castle Rock and Larkspur and up Monument Hill, every one of them sharp. Three nights ago this ridge had been under a gray lid. Tonight it was a ridge with a highway on it, and she watched the highway the whole way over the top anyway.`,
    `Pueblo tower cleared them to land at 5:56. On the ramp, through the FBO window, she could see a woman in a quilted coat holding a foil-covered pan with both hands like a casserole at a funeral, which was her aunt, who'd driven out to the airport because Jordan's mother had called her. The flan had the burnt part on top. Abby ate two pieces standing up in the lobby and said it was the best dispatch release she'd ever been handed.`,
    `Jordan called her mother from the ramp, in the cold, with the beacon going white and green over her head.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Ma. I'm in Pueblo."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"I know. Your tia called me from the parking lot."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Was it a good flight?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"It was a clear flight."</span> Which was the honest answer, and her mother seemed to understand it was a different thing from a beautiful night. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Call me from Centennial. Not the car. Centennial."</span>`,
    `Home at ten thousand five hundred, two full stops, 7:50 at the tie down. Two point oh, all of it night, 160 nautical miles. In the logbook, under the night line, Abby drew a box and put a check in it with the green pen, and then she capped the pen and looked at Jordan over the wing.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's the night. Book the written. January. Text me the date before you click anything."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: the rules, and the room you take them in`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Sixty questions, two hours`,
        body: [`Here's the test, straight from the FAA's testing matrix, the version revised in September. The Private Pilot Airplane test, code PAR, is 60 questions in 2.0 hours, and you pass with 70 percent. That's 42 right. Every question has three choices. The FAA also slips in a few validation questions it's trying out. They don't count for or against you, you can't tell which ones they are, and the two hours already includes the time to answer them. So answer every question like it counts.`,
          `You'll see 2.5 hours in older books. You'll even see it in a table at the back of the private pilot ACS. The FAA cut the time for this test on April 24, 2023, and the matrix is the newer document. It says two. When two FAA papers disagree about a number, look at the dates.`,
          `Some questions need a chart or a graph. Some of those now show the picture right on the screen with the question, because the FAA and PSI, the company that runs knowledge testing for it, have been building the images into the questions. Others still send you to the supplement book, FAA-CT-8080-2H, the Airman Knowledge Testing Supplement for Sport Pilot, Recreational Pilot, Remote Pilot, and Private Pilot. It's from 2018, and some of its sectional excerpts are printed small enough to make a grown woman squint. The proctor hands it to you and takes it back, and checks it for marks, so don't write in it. The FAA says the books stay in the testing centers until it announces a removal date, with plenty of notice.`,
          `Two hours for sixty questions is two minutes each. Most of them take thirty seconds. The ones with a figure take five minutes. It comes out about even, and you'll have time left if you don't stall.`],
        terms: [[`PAR`,`Private Pilot Airplane knowledge test: 60 questions, 2.0 hours, 70 percent to pass (Testing Matrix, 9/25/26).`],[`Validation questions`,`Unscored items mixed in. Not part of the 60, and their time is included in the 2.0 hours.`],[`FAA-CT-8080-2H`,`The 2018 testing supplement for sport, recreational, remote, and private tests. Some figures are now built into the questions instead.`]],
        exam: `Passing is 70 percent. If a book says 2.5 hours, the book is old.` },
      { h: `Getting in the door`,
        body: [`Three things get you a seat. The first is an FAA Tracking Number. You got one in May, when we did your student certificate in IACRA, and it prints on your score report. You register with it on PSI's website and pick a testing center.`,
          `The second is identification. 61.35 wants a photograph, a signature, and a date of birth that shows you'll meet the age requirement before the report expires. The matrix adds that the ID has to be valid and current and show your physical residential address. A Colorado driver's license with your apartment on it does all of that. A post office box isn't a residential address. If your license doesn't show where you live, the matrix lists other proof you can bring, like a utility bill or a signed lease. Read the list before you go, because not every bill counts.`,
          `The third is authorization. 61.35(a)(1) says you need an endorsement from an instructor that you've done the ground training or a home-study course and you're prepared. 61.103(d) says it again from the private pilot side. AC 61-65K has the wording in Appendix A. For your test it's A.36: I certify you've received the training in 61.105, and I've determined you're prepared for the knowledge test. If you'd studied on your own, I could review your home study and sign A.86 instead. Either way it's my signature in your logbook, and the logbook comes with you.`,
          `61.105(b) is the list of what the test covers, thirteen items long, and it's worth reading once so nothing surprises you: the regulations, NTSB reporting, the AIM and advisory circulars, charts and navigation, radio procedures, weather, collision avoidance and wake turbulence, density altitude, weight and balance, aerodynamics and systems, stalls and spins, decision making, and preflight action including alternatives. That's the menu. You've eaten all of it.`,
          `What you can carry in: a plotter, a flight computer like an E6B, a straightedge, and a calculator, as long as you clear its memory in front of the proctor before and after. No written material of any kind, and that includes anything written on your tools. What the manufacturer printed on an E6B is fine. Your notes on it aren't. They give you scratch paper, count it out, and count it back. Bathroom breaks are allowed with an escort, and the clock keeps running while you're gone.`],
        terms: [[`FTN`,`FAA Tracking Number from IACRA. Needed to register; printed on the report.`],[`61.35(a)`,`Prerequisites: instructor endorsement, and ID with photo, signature, date of birth, and a residential address (61.35 and the Matrix).`],[`A.36`,`AC 61-65K endorsement for the private pilot knowledge test (61.35(a)(1), 61.103(d), 61.105).`],[`Allowed aids`,`Plotter, flight computer, straightedge, calculator with memory cleared. No written materials.`]],
        exam: `To take a knowledge test you need an instructor's endorsement and proper identification. Not a medical, and not a solo endorsement.` },
      { h: `The report, and the codes on it`,
        body: [`When you finish, your score comes up on the screen, and the proctor prints an Airman Knowledge Test Report with an embossed seal on it. Everybody calls it the AKTR. It has your name, your FTN, your score, and a list of codes for the questions you missed. On the private test those are ACS codes.`,
          `Read one with me. PA.I.C.K2. PA is the private pilot airplane ACS. I is the area of operation, Preflight Preparation. C is the task, Weather Information. K2 is the second knowledge element in that task. The code tells you where the question came from. It doesn't tell you the question, and nobody ever will. You go back and learn the whole element.`,
          `That paper matters two more times. 61.39(a)(1) says you have to have passed the knowledge test within the 24 calendar months before the month you finish the practical test. Pass in January 2027 and you're good through January 31, 2029. And 61.39(a)(6) says that before your checkride I have to sign that you've had training within the 2 calendar months before the month you apply, that you're ready, and that you've shown me you know the areas you missed on the test. Those are endorsements A.1 and A.2. The ACS then tells the examiner that the oral has to include whatever you missed. So the codes on that report are the first page of your checkride.`,
          `Keep the original, in your logbook, not your glovebox. If you lose it, the FAA says PSI can print a duplicate for any test taken on or after January 13, 2020. Fail the test and 61.49 says you need more training from an instructor and an endorsement that you're ready, and you bring the failed report with you. Pass it and hate your score, and the matrix lets you sit it again with the passing report as your authorization, after 30 days, and you hand in the old report. The new score is the one that counts, even if it's lower. Most people find better uses for a Tuesday.`],
        terms: [[`AKTR`,`Airman Knowledge Test Report: score, plus ACS codes for missed questions. Keep the original for the checkride.`],[`ACS code`,`PA.I.C.K2 = ACS, area of operation, task, element (K knowledge, R risk management, S skill).`],[`61.39(a)(1)`,`Knowledge test passed within the 24 calendar months before the month the practical test is completed.`],[`A.1 and A.2`,`Checkride prerequisites: prep training within 2 calendar months, prepared, and missed areas reviewed (61.39(a)(6)).`],[`61.49`,`Retest after failure: more training and an instructor endorsement.`]],
        exam: `Expect the 24 calendar month window, and the instructor's review of your missed areas before the practical test.` },
      { h: `What a private pilot can do: 61.113`,
        body: [`Now the regulations. You'll get more of these on the test than anything else, so we'll go slow, starting with the one you'll live under for the rest of your life.`,
          `61.113(a): a private pilot can't act as pilot in command of an aircraft carrying passengers or property for compensation or hire, and can't be paid to act as pilot in command at all. Then come the exceptions. You can fly in connection with your business or job, if the flight is only incidental to it and nobody's paying to ride or to ship something. You can fly a charitable, nonprofit, or community event flight that follows 91.146. You can be reimbursed for fuel, oil, airport costs, or rental on a search and location flight run by a government agency or a search organization. An aircraft salesman with 200 hours can demonstrate an airplane to a buyer. And paragraph (i) is BasicMed, which is a different afternoon.`,
          `Paragraph (c) is the one everybody asks about. A private pilot may not pay less than the pro rata share of the operating expenses of a flight with passengers, and the only expenses that count are fuel, oil, airport expenditures, and rental fees. Pro rata means split evenly among everyone aboard, the pilot included. Three people, you pay at least a third. You can pay more. You can't pay less.`,
          `AC 61-142 has the fine print, and it's short. Insurance, maintenance, depreciation, charts: not shareable. If it's not one of those four things, the pilot pays it. The FAA has also read the rule for decades to require a common purpose. You need your own reason to go to that place, and you're the one who picks it. Your cousin asks you to fly him to Grand Junction to get his truck, and he'll buy the gas? No common purpose. You're flying to your mother's birthday dinner and your sister-in-law rides along to the same dinner? That's one. And you can't advertise seats to the public, which the AC calls holding out. There's a line in there you'll like. Compensation doesn't have to be money. The AC lists piling up flight time as a kind of compensation. So when you're a private pilot and somebody offers you free use of their airplane if you'll just fly them to Vegas, that isn't free.`],
        terms: [[`61.113(a)`,`No acting as PIC for compensation or hire, and no carrying passengers or property for hire.`],[`61.113(c)`,`The pilot pays at least a pro rata share of fuel, oil, airport expenditures, or rental fees.`],[`Common purpose`,`AC 61-142: the pilot has their own reason to go and chooses the destination.`],[`Holding out`,`Offering rides to the public. It turns expense sharing into common carriage.`]],
        exam: `Pro rata questions are division. Add up the shareable costs, divide by everyone aboard including the pilot, and that's the least the pilot pays.` },
      { h: `Staying legal after the checkride: 61.56 and 61.57`,
        body: [`61.56(c) says you can't act as pilot in command unless, since the beginning of the 24th calendar month before the month you're flying, you've done a flight review and had it endorsed. A flight review is at least an hour of ground and an hour of flight, covering the Part 91 rules and whatever maneuvers the instructor decides you need to show. Calendar months, again. A review signed any day in December 2024 is good through December 31, 2026. On January 1 it isn't.`,
          `Two outs that matter to you. A checkride counts as one, so your private checkride is your first flight review, and your 24 months start the month you pass. And 61.56(g) says a student pilot in training with a current solo endorsement doesn't need one, which is why I've never brought it up. There's a third, the WINGS program in 61.56(e), for later.`,
          `To carry anybody, 61.57(a) wants three takeoffs and three landings in the preceding 90 days, as the sole manipulator of the controls, in the same category and class, and type if a type rating is required. In a tricycle gear airplane touch and goes count. In a tailwheel airplane they have to be full stops. You know the night version from December: three takeoffs and three landings to a full stop between an hour after sunset and an hour before sunrise.`,
          `Currency is the legal floor and nothing else. The ACS asks you about proficiency versus currency, PA.I.A.R1, and you already know which one I care about. Three landings in 90 days makes it legal to take Ellie. It doesn't make you ready for a gusty crosswind at Akron.`],
        terms: [[`61.56`,`Flight review: 1 hour ground and 1 hour flight within 24 calendar months. A checkride counts, and so does a WINGS phase (61.56(e)). Students with current solo endorsements are exempt.`],[`61.57(a)`,`Passengers: 3 takeoffs and 3 landings in 90 days, same category, class, and type if required. Tailwheel: full stops.`],[`PA.I.A.R1`,`Proficiency versus currency. Legal recency is a minimum, not a measure of skill.`]],
        exam: `Flight review math runs to the end of the month. A review from any day in June 2025 is good through June 30, 2027.` },
      { h: `Medicals, what's in your pocket, and your address`,
        body: [`61.23 again, because the test will ask it with a different birthday. A third class medical used for private privileges lasts until the end of the 60th month after the month of the exam if you were under 40 when you took it, and the 24th month if you were 40 or older. A first or second class used for private flying follows the same 60 and 24. The higher privileges run out sooner. A first class taken at 38 is good for airline captain work for 12 months and for your kind of flying for 60.`,
          `Yours was April 2026, at 35, so it runs through April 30, 2031. Walt's last one was in March 2025, well after he turned 40, so it's on the 24 month clock and runs through the end of March 2027. He booked the next exam in December. Of course he did.`,
          `61.53 is the one people confuse with it. You can't act as pilot in command while you know or have reason to know of a medical condition that would make you unable to meet the requirements for your medical, or while you're taking a medication or getting a treatment that would. It's about disqualifying conditions and medications. Everything smaller is IMSAFE's job.`,
          `When you're pilot in command, 61.3 wants three things on you or easy to reach in the airplane: your pilot certificate, a photo ID like a driver's license, and your medical. If you fly under BasicMed, a valid U.S. driver's license takes the medical's place, and the BasicMed checklist and course certificate stay in your logbook. On a solo cross-country, 61.51(i)(2) also wants your logbook and student certificate aboard, which is why your logbook has lived in the flight bag since November. And you show your logbook to the FAA, the NTSB, or a police officer who asks reasonably. 61.51(b) is what goes in it: date, total time, where you left and landed, the aircraft type and N number, and what kind of time it was: solo, pilot in command, training received, day or night, actual or simulated instrument.`,
          `Move, and 61.60 gives you 30 days to tell the FAA's Airman Certification Branch, in writing. After that you can't use your certificate until you do. The regulation even prints the post office box in Oklahoma City, which I find kind of charming.`],
        terms: [[`61.23(d)`,`Private privileges: the medical lasts to the end of the 60th month (under 40 at the exam) or the 24th month (40 or older).`],[`61.53`,`No PIC with a known disqualifying condition or medication.`],[`61.3`,`PIC carries pilot certificate, photo ID, and medical (or, under BasicMed, a U.S. driver's license).`],[`61.60`,`Change of address: notify in writing within 30 days, or stop using the certificate.`]],
        exam: `Medical questions give an age at the exam and a date. Find the privileges and the age, then count months to the end of the month.` },
      { h: `The airplane's paperwork`,
        body: [`91.7 is short. Nobody flies an airplane that isn't airworthy, the pilot in command decides whether it's in condition for safe flight, and the pilot ends the flight when something unairworthy shows up. You're the last inspector every single time, and Marisol's signature doesn't change that.`,
          `In the airplane, 91.203 wants the airworthiness certificate displayed at the cabin or cockpit entrance where people can read it, and an effective registration certificate. Under 47.40 a registration now lasts seven years from the last day of the month it was issued. 47.41 ends one early in a few cases: the airplane's sold, or destroyed, or 30 days have passed since the death of the person it's registered to. 91.9 wants the operating limitations, which means the approved flight manual or POH, the markings, and the placards, and then there's the weight and balance data for that exact airplane. ARROW, with the extra R, the radio station license, only needed now when you fly to another country.`,
          `Biscuit's paperwork is in the plastic sleeve on the left door post. The POH is in the seat pocket, and inside its back cover is the copy of Marisol's 2019 weight and balance you put there in August, where Danny's old 1,438 pound card used to be. The maintenance logbooks live in Marisol's cabinet, not in the airplane, which is where they belong.`],
        terms: [[`91.7`,`No operating an unairworthy aircraft. The PIC determines whether it's in condition for safe flight.`],[`91.203`,`Airworthiness certificate (displayed) and registration aboard.`],[`47.40 and 47.41`,`Registration lasts 7 years. It ends early on sale, destruction, or 30 days after the holder's death.`],[`ARROW`,`Airworthiness, registration, radio license (international only), operating limitations, weight and balance.`]],
        exam: `Which document isn't required on board? The maintenance records. They stay in the hangar.` },
      { h: `Inspections, records, and ADs`,
        body: [`You did this in May with the cowling off, so here's the review. The annual, 91.409(a), within the preceding 12 calendar months, signed by an A&P with an inspection authorization. The 100 hour, 91.409(b), only if the airplane carries people for hire or someone gives flight instruction for hire in an airplane that person provides, and you can run 10 hours over to reach a shop, which comes out of the next 100. The transponder, 91.413, every 24 calendar months. The ELT, 91.207: inspected every 12 calendar months, and the battery replaced after more than one cumulative hour of use or when half its useful life is gone, with the new date marked on the outside of the unit and in the records. 91.207(f) even exempts training flights that stay within 50 nautical miles. Biscuit has one anyway, because Biscuit goes to Pueblo.`,
          `91.417 is what has to be kept. Records of maintenance and inspections, each with a description of the work, the date, and the signature and certificate number of whoever returned the airplane to service. Those can go once the work is repeated or superseded, or after a year. Then the records that live forever and go with the airplane when it's sold: total time on the airframe, engine, and propeller, the status of life-limited parts, time since overhaul, the current inspection status, and the current status of every applicable airworthiness directive, with its number and revision date, the method of compliance, and, if it recurs, when it's due next.`,
          `Part 39 calls ADs legally enforceable rules for aircraft, engines, propellers, and appliances, and 39.7 says operating a product that doesn't meet an applicable AD is a violation. A service bulletin from the manufacturer is advice. An AD is the FAA. Some are one time, some recur, and each one has a compliance time, usually hours or calendar time, whichever comes first. Inside that window the airplane's legal to fly. 91.403(a) makes the owner or operator primarily responsible for keeping the airplane airworthy, ADs included. You're an owner now. That's partly your name on the list.`,
          `After any maintenance, 91.407 says the airplane needs a return to service by someone authorized, and the logbook entry, before anybody flies it. If the work might have changed how it flies, a private pilot or better has to fly it, check it, and log it before passengers ride.`],
        terms: [[`91.409`,`Annual every 12 calendar months. 100 hour only for hire, plus 10 to reach a shop.`],[`91.207`,`ELT inspected every 12 calendar months; battery after 1 cumulative hour or half its useful life.`],[`91.417(a)(2)(v)`,`AD status: the AD number and revision date, method of compliance, and next due time for recurring ADs, kept with the aircraft.`],[`Part 39`,`ADs are legally enforceable rules (39.3). Operating out of compliance is a violation (39.7).`]],
        exam: `ADs are mandatory, and the owner or operator is primarily responsible for them. The pilot still decides before each flight whether the airplane is airworthy.` },
      { h: `When something happens: NTSB 830`,
        body: [`This one's in Title 49, not 14, and it's the one paramedics get wrong, so listen. 49 CFR 830 is the NTSB's rule on notifying and reporting.`,
          `An aircraft accident is an occurrence associated with operating an aircraft, between the time anyone boards with the intention of flight and the time everyone has gotten off, in which somebody dies or is seriously injured or the aircraft takes substantial damage. A fatal injury is one that causes death within 30 days.`,
          `Serious injury has a definition, and it isn't the one you use in the back of a rig. It means hospitalization for more than 48 hours, starting within 7 days of the injury. Or a fracture of any bone, except simple fractures of fingers, toes, or the nose. Or severe hemorrhage, or nerve, muscle, or tendon damage. Or anything involving an internal organ. Or second or third degree burns, or any burns over more than 5 percent of the body. A broken wrist is serious. A simple broken finger isn't, under 830.2, no matter what the person holding it says.`,
          `Substantial damage is damage or failure that hurts the structural strength, performance, or flight characteristics, and would normally need a major repair or replacement. Then comes the list of what doesn't count, which the test adores: an engine failure or damage limited to one engine, bent fairings or cowling, dented skin, small punctured holes, ground damage to propeller blades, and damage to landing gear, wheels, tires, flaps, engine accessories, brakes, or wingtips.`,
          `830.5 says you notify the nearest NTSB office immediately, by the fastest means you've got, of any accident and of a list of serious incidents. The ones that fit an airplane like Biscuit: a flight control system malfunction or failure, a required crewmember who can't do the job because of injury or illness, an in-flight fire, a collision in flight, damage to property other than the aircraft estimated at more than $25,000, release of all or part of a propeller blade other than from ground contact alone, and an overdue aircraft believed to be in an accident. There are more for big airplanes and glass panels. Notice what isn't on it. An engine failure by itself.`,
          `830.15 is the written report, on NTSB Form 6120.1/2, due within 10 days after an accident, or after 7 days if an overdue aircraft is still missing. For an incident on the list, you send a report only if the NTSB asks. 830.10 says preserve the wreckage and the records, and don't move anything except to get people out, to protect the wreckage, or to protect the public. If you have to move something, take photos and notes first.`,
          `One more, from Part 91. 91.25 says the FAA won't use what you send to NASA's Aviation Safety Reporting Program against you in an enforcement action, except for accidents and crimes. When you mess up and nobody gets hurt, write it down and send it. It's the grown-up version of Danny's notebook.`],
        terms: [[`Aircraft accident (830.2)`,`Death, serious injury, or substantial damage between boarding for flight and everyone getting off.`],[`Serious injury`,`Hospitalized over 48 hours, starting within 7 days; fractures except simple fingers, toes, nose; severe bleeding; nerve, muscle, or tendon damage; internal organs; 2nd or 3rd degree burns, or burns over 5 percent.`],[`830.5`,`Immediate notification: accidents, plus listed incidents such as in-flight fire, flight control failure, midair, property damage over $25,000.`],[`830.15`,`Report within 10 days of an accident; 7 days if an overdue aircraft is still missing; incidents only on request.`]],
        exam: `Expect to sort events into accident, notifiable incident, or neither. A flat tire or a dented wingtip isn't substantial damage.` },
      { h: `After you land: postflight`,
        body: [`Area XII is the part of the checkride nobody practices, because the flying's over. The ACS wants the shutdown and securing done by checklist, a postflight inspection, and any discrepancies and servicing needs written down. It also names the risks: distractions, the airport's security rules, and passengers wandering around a ramp full of propellers.`,
          `Here's how I want it. Before the engine stops, listen on the emergency frequency for a few seconds. The AIM says hard landings set off ELTs, and you'd rather know before you walk away than when somebody at the FBO hears it and calls Marisol. Mixture, mags, master, by the checklist. Control lock in. Chocks, then the left wing rope, the right wing, and the tail, snug but not tight as a guitar string, with knots you can untie in January with gloves on. Pitot cover. Then walk around her again like it's a preflight, because it's the next pilot's preflight. Oil, blue stains from fuel, new dents, tires.`,
          `Whatever you find goes on the squawk sheet in the binder, in words a stranger could act on, with the date and your name. If it might ground the airplane, tell Marisol and whoever has Biscuit next, tonight, not when it's convenient. Passengers get out after the propeller stops, on the side away from it, and you walk them off the ramp. The flight isn't over until the next person can trust the airplane.`],
        terms: [[`PA.XII.A`,`After landing, parking, and securing: checklists, postflight inspection, documented discrepancies, airplane secured.`],[`Before shutdown`,`Listen on the emergency frequency for an accidental ELT activation (AIM 6-2-4).`],[`Squawk`,`A written discrepancy, for the mechanic and the next pilot.`]],
        exam: `Postflight is rare on the written and certain on the checkride. Know the sequence, and know discrepancies get written down.` },
      { h: `How to take it`,
        body: [`Last part, and it's the one I wish somebody had told me. Sleep the night before. You know that. You also know your schedule. No solo the morning after a 24. Treat the written like a solo.`,
          `Read the last line of each question first, because that's the actual question. Then read the whole thing and try to answer before you look at the choices. With three choices, one is usually silly, one is close, and one is right. The close one is the trap, and it's close on purpose, often with a number borrowed from the wrong regulation.`,
          `Use the figure every time, even when you're sure you remember what it says. If a question stops you cold, choose the best answer you've got, mark it, and move on. A blank is wrong. A guess has a one in three chance.`,
          `When you've answered all sixty, go back to the marked ones. Change an answer only when you can say why, in your head, with a regulation number in the sentence. A feeling isn't a reason.`,
          `And study the reasons, not the answers. The question banks are out there and people memorize them, and then the FAA changes one number and they pick the old answer. If you can't explain why the right one is right, you don't know it yet. You just recognize it.`],
        terms: [[`Two minutes`,`120 minutes ÷ 60 questions. Most take far less.`],[`Mark and move`,`Answer, flag it for review, keep going. Unanswered is wrong.`],[`Changing answers`,`Only with a specific reason you can name.`]],
        exam: `None of this is on the test. It's how you get through it.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f11s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`Two in the morning`, q:`Four test slots are open, and Jordan's 24 hour shifts start every third day. Which date should she book?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, December 18, 2:10 in the morning, the station kitchen, the quiet stretch of a twenty four. The microwave clock was four minutes fast and nobody had fixed it since the summer. Jordan had the PSI site open on her phone, logged in with the FAA Tracking Number that IACRA had handed her in May with the student certificate, and a testing center picked out in an office park off Arapahoe Road, fifteen minutes from her apartment.`,`The January calendar for that center showed four open slots. Friday the 8th at eight in the morning. Tuesday the 12th at nine. Saturday the 23rd at one. Thursday, February 4, at ten.`,`Taped to the station fridge, under a magnet shaped like a defibrillator that somebody's kid had made, was the shift calendar. Her January 24s started on the 1st, the 4th, the 7th, the 10th, and the 13th, seven to seven. Abby wanted a date before Jordan clicked anything.`,`${(G.pushedweather)?`She'd been up I-25 at night twice now. The first time the headlights had gone soft over Larkspur and then gone out. Wednesday they'd stayed sharp all the way over Monument Hill, and she'd still caught herself counting them, the way you count respirations on a patient you already coded once.`:`${(G.nopersonalmins)?`Wednesday night over Monument Hill she'd thought about the index card she never wrote, the one that was supposed to go where Danny kept the picture of Ellie. The spot on the panel was still empty.`:`Wednesday night over Monument Hill she'd checked the line at the bottom of her card twice. CEILING 5,000 AGL ALL THE WAY. The ceiling had been the moon.`}`}`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Friday, January 8, at eight, the first open slot.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She texts it to Abby. The answer comes at 6:40, which is when Abby wakes up. <b>The 8th is the morning after the 7th. You'd walk in at 8 after 24 hours awake. No solo the morning after a 24. This is a solo. Pick again.</b> Jordan reads it twice and picks the 12th.`,
          why:`Fatigue degrades exactly what a knowledge test measures. Abby's rule is no solo the morning after a 24, and a test taken after 24 hours on duty belongs under the same rule.`},
        {t:`Tuesday, January 12, at nine, after a full night off.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She counts it twice on the fridge. The 10th ends at seven on the 11th, she sleeps the 11th, and the 12th is the first clear day. She blocks the 4th through the 11th in her calendar for review, around the shifts, and texts Abby the date and a screenshot. Abby answers at 6:40. <b>Good. That's a date you'll arrive rested for. Endorsement on the 6th if you earn it.</b>`,
          why:`Picking a date around her shift cycle protects sleep, and blocking the week before makes the review real. A firm date also leaves room afterward for the instructor's review of missed areas and the checkride prep before the April deadline.`},
        {t:`None yet. Wait until Abby says she's ready, then pick a date.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<b>I'm saying it now,</b> Abby writes at 6:40. <b>Ready isn't a feeling that shows up on its own. It's a date you study toward. Pick one.</b> Jordan looks at the calendar again and picks the 12th.`,
          why:`Waiting for the instructor's go-ahead is reasonable, but she had already been told to book in January. A test with no date tends to slide, and the AKTR is a prerequisite for the checkride and the instructor reviews that come after it.`},
        {t:`Thursday, February 4, closer to the checkride and the oral.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Abby calls her at 6:45, which Abby never does. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The report has to exist before Walt can book a slot, and I have to go over your misses with you and sign for it before the checkride. February 4 pushes all of that toward April. The clock doesn't care how fresh you feel."</span> Jordan books the 12th while Abby's still on the phone.`,
          why:`The AKTR is valid for 24 calendar months, so freshness isn't a reason to wait. Booking late compresses the deficiency review and checkride prep that 61.39(a)(6) requires, against a deadline that won't move.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s2`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`XII`, title:`The blue stain`, q:`Postflight, Jordan finds a blue stain and a cracked gasket at the left fuel cap. What should she do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, December 22. The long one. KAPA to Limon, 57.7 miles, the leg she'd log as her one over fifty in a straight line. Limon to Pueblo, 70.8. Pueblo home, 78.6. Two hundred and seven nautical miles, three full stops, flight following on every leg, the limits from Abby's November endorsement copied onto the top of her kneeboard, and a new endorsement for today written underneath them in green.`,`Limon had a 4,700 foot runway, a self-serve pump that took a card, a windsock frozen stiff at a forty five degree angle, and two deer standing in the grass by the end of 34 like they'd been hired. Pueblo had a tower and a wind out of the north that made the last leg home take longer than it had any right to. She was on the ground at Centennial at 12:14, with forty six minutes to spare on Abby's one o'clock line.`,`At the tie down she did it the way Abby taught it. Before the engine stopped she listened on the emergency frequency for a few seconds. Nothing but hiss. Mixture, mags, master. Control lock. Chocks, left rope, right rope, tail. Pitot cover. Then the walkaround again, slowly, because tomorrow at seven it would be Walt's preflight.`,`The left fuel cap had a blue stain fanning back from it across the top of the wing, a thin streak of dyed fuel that had dried in the wind. She unscrewed the cap. The rubber gasket under it had a crack running halfway around. The left gauge had been reading low since Pueblo, and she'd blamed the gauge.`,`Inside the hangar, on the shelf under the whiteboard with Danny's capitals still on it, sat the coffee can of spare fuel caps.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Swap in a spare cap from the coffee can, and have Biscuit ready for Walt.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She has the old cap off and a spare in her hand when Marisol's truck pulls up, early, for something else. Marisol looks at the cap, then at the stain, then at Jordan. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Good find. Wrong next step. I want to know why that seal went before somebody covers it up, and I want it in the book. Give me that."</span> She writes it up herself, swaps the cap, and texts Walt a picture.`,
          why:`Swapping the cap removes the immediate hazard, but the ACS postflight standard is to document the discrepancy, and a mechanic should see what failed and record the fix. An undocumented swap leaves the next pilot and the records in the dark.`},
        {t:`Write it on the squawk sheet and text Marisol a photo.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She writes it in the binder in block capitals: <b>12/22, 1225. LEFT FUEL CAP GASKET CRACKED. FUEL STAIN AFT OF CAP ON WING. LEFT GAUGE READ LOW AFTER KPUB. J. REYES.</b> Photo to Marisol, photo to Walt. Marisol answers in four minutes. <b>Don't touch it. Coming.</b> She's there in twenty, puts a new cap on from the coffee can, and writes a line in Biscuit's logbook. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Danny bought that whole can at a swap meet in 2019. Said Biscuit eats fuel caps. She does."</span> Walt texts back one word, <b>Good.</b>, which from Walt is a medal.`,
          why:`PA.XII.A: a postflight inspection, documented discrepancies, and a secured airplane. Writing the squawk so a stranger could act on it and telling the mechanic and the next pilot right away is how a postflight protects the next flight.`},
        {t:`Dip the tanks to rule out a leak, and mention it to Marisol next time.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She dips the tanks, decides it's probably just the cap, and goes home. At 6:55 the next morning Walt texts the club chat a picture of the blue stain with no comment at all, and Marisol answers before Jordan's awake. By the time Jordan sees it, it's handled, and nobody's said her name, which is worse.`,
          why:`A discrepancy that might affect the next flight has to be documented and passed on before the next pilot's preflight, not whenever it's convenient. The next pilot shouldn't have to rediscover it.`},
        {t:`Leave it as is. She landed with plenty of fuel, and the cap held.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She's halfway to the car when the stain bothers her enough to turn around. A cap that leaks into the slipstream is a tank that empties faster than the gauge or the plan says. She goes back and writes it up.`,
          why:`A leaking fuel cap can siphon fuel in flight, and a fuel plan built on burn rate won't know about it. Postflight discrepancies get documented and reported, even when the flight that found them ended fine.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s3`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`Do I count`, q:`Claire and Ellie want to pay for the Akron trip. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, December 25, Claire's kitchen in Parker. Tamales steaming in the big pot, the good tablecloth, a Christmas playlist that had already looped twice. Ellie's present to Jordan had been a luggage tag for the flight bag that said PILOT IN COMMAND in silver glitter glue, which was early by at least one checkride, and which Jordan had clipped on the strap immediately.`,`After the dishes Claire put an envelope on the counter. On the front, in her handwriting, AKRON GAS. Inside, a stack of twenties.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Claire" data-tone="Quiet, firm, warm.">"For whenever it is. Danny never once let me pay for anything on that airplane. I want to pay for this one."</span>`,`Ellie came in from the living room with a sandwich bag of quarters and set it on top of the envelope. Six dollars. She'd counted it twice in front of everybody.`,`Jordan had read 61.113 four times that week. She looked at the envelope.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take the envelope with thanks, and let Claire cover this one.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She puts it in the flight bag. That night, reading the AC again, she gets to the line about the pilot not paying less than her share, and the envelope is sitting right there next to the green notebook. She texts Claire. <b>I have to give some of this back. It's a rule. A real one.</b> Claire: <b>Of course it is.</b>`,
          why:`61.113(c) requires a private pilot to pay at least their pro rata share of the shareable operating expenses. If a passenger covers all of it, the pilot is paying less than their share and receiving compensation.`},
        {t:`Thank Claire, and explain that the pilot has to pay at least a third.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She explains it on the back of the envelope with a pen from the junk drawer. Fuel, oil, airport fees. Three people aboard, so she pays at least a third. Claire can pay the rest of those, if she wants, but not insurance, not the monthly payment, not maintenance. Ellie reads it upside down. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Do I count? I'm a person."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"You count. You're a third."</span> Ellie considers this and pushes the quarters toward Claire. <span class="said" data-who="Ellie">"Then Mom pays mine."</span> Claire laughs, the real one, the one Jordan hasn't heard since summer, and takes back two of the twenties without being asked.`,
          why:`61.113(c): the pilot pays no less than her pro rata share, counting everyone aboard, and only fuel, oil, airport expenditures, and rental fees can be shared (AC 61-142). The trip is Jordan's own, to a place she chose, so there's a common purpose.`},
        {t:`Hand it back, and pay for the whole trip herself.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Claire takes the envelope back and puts it on top of the fridge, which in that house means it isn't over. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"Legally that's fine, I'm sure. It's also exactly what he used to do."</span> She doesn't say it unkindly. Jordan explains the actual rule, and Claire gets the envelope back down.`,
          why:`Paying everything herself is legal, since the rule sets a floor on the pilot's share, not a ceiling. But Claire is allowed to share fuel, oil, and fees, and wanting to was the point.`},
        {t:`Split everything three ways for that month, the gas and her $410 share payment.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Claire does the math on her phone and frowns. <span class="said" data-who="Claire">"That's a lot of gas."</span> Jordan looks at the number and realizes what's in it: insurance, the hangar, Marisol's reserve. She pulls up AC 61-142 on her phone and reads the list out loud. Four things. Gas, oil, airport fees, rental. The $410 isn't any of them.`,
          why:`Only fuel, oil, airport expenditures, and rental fees can be shared under 61.113(c). Insurance, maintenance, and other fixed costs of ownership must be paid by the pilot.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s4`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`Lone Tree`, q:`Russ asks if his flight review and his move to Lone Tree are fine. What should Jordan say?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, December 28, 9:14 AM, the club chat.`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="9:14 AM">booked biscuit jan 1, 8 am, pancakes in pueblo with the brother in law. new years tradition starting now</prop>`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="9:16 AM">q for the paramedic since she reads everything. my flight review was dec 2024 i think, the 12th, with the guy from the north end. two years is two years right. also i moved to lone tree nov 20, does the faa need to know or is that a vibe</prop>`,`Jordan was on her couch with coffee and the Part 61 printout she'd been marking up all weekend. She'd just highlighted 61.56(c) in orange.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say nothing, and trust that Russ knows his own dates.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She puts the phone down. Forty minutes later Walt posts in the chat: <b>Russ. 61.56. Call Abby.</b> Then he texts Jordan, just her. <b>You saw it first. Next time say so. That's how this club works.</b>`,
          why:`Every pilot is responsible for their own currency, but in a club, the person who notices a problem says something. Holding back to avoid seeming presumptuous lets a legal problem become a flight.`},
        {t:`Text Walt privately, and let the safety officer take it to Russ.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Walt answers in a minute. <b>You found it. You tell him. I'll back you.</b> So she does, in the chat, with the section numbers. Walt posts a single thumbs up under it, which from Walt is a parade.`,
          why:`Going through the safety officer is reasonable, and it works. But she had the answer and the regulation in hand, and Russ had asked her directly. A clear, polite answer helped most.`},
        {t:`Tell him the calendar months run through January, so his January 1 flight is fine.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`She types it. Before she sends it she reads the paragraph one more time, the way she'd read a dose back. Since the beginning of the 24th calendar month before the month in which that pilot acts. She does the count on her fingers, deletes the message, and writes the right one.`,
          why:`61.56(c) counts calendar months. A review in December 2024 covers flights through December 31, 2026. On January 1, 2027, it no longer counts, regardless of the day it was signed.`},
        {t:`Tell him he's good through December 31, and his address notice is late.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<b>61.56(c): Dec 2024 review covers you through Dec 31. Jan 1 you're not current. Abby has slots. 61.60: you had 30 days to tell the FAA your new address, in writing. Nov 20 plus 30 was Dec 20. Have you flown since?</b> Three dots for a long time. <b>i was in phoenix. so no</b>. Then: <b>in WRITING. like a letter</b>. <b>The reg has a PO box in Oklahoma City.</b> <b>i'm going to mail the faa a christmas card</b>. Abby, a minute later: <b>Russ. Wednesday at ten. 1.0 ground, 1.0 flight. Bring donuts. That's my fee on top of my fee.</b>`,
          why:`61.56(c) runs to the end of the 24th calendar month, so a December 2024 review expires after December 31, 2026. 61.60 bars using the certificate more than 30 days after a move until the FAA is notified in writing, and notifying fixes it going forward.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s5`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.B`, title:`The selector`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday, January 3, 7:40 in the morning, the hangar. Jordan had a dual lesson at nine, hood work and steep turns, the last of her three instrument hours. Russ had flown his review with Abby on the 30th and Pueblo on New Year's Day, and had brought back a box of pastries with one missing, which he swore was quality control.`,`Marisol had a printout on the workbench with a coffee ring already on it. An airworthiness directive, new, effective December 28, for the fuel selector valves on a range of 172s from the late seventies. Biscuit's serial number was in the range. Inspect the valve detent and the shaft seal within the next 50 hours time in service or 6 months, whichever came first, then every 100 hours.`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="7:32 AM">is biscuit grounded? asking for me. and for the pastries</prop>`,`Marisol didn't look up from her coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Well? You're an owner. You tell me."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Cancel today. An open AD on the fuel selector means nobody should fly her.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Marisol shrugs. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"You can. Nobody'll fight you on the fuel selector."</span> She taps the printout. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"But read the compliance time. Fifty hours or six months. The FAA's saying it's legal to fly inside that window, and I'm doing it Tuesday anyway. You'd be grounding her for a rule that already gave you until June."</span> Jordan reads it, and flies.`,
          why:`Grounding is always allowed, but an AD's compliance time sets when the action must be done. Inside that window the airplane is legal, and knowing how to read the compliance time is the skill being tested.`},
        {t:`Read the AD's compliance time, then check the logbook hours against it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She reads it like a drug insert. Applicability: Biscuit's in. Compliance: 50 hours time in service or 6 months after December 28, whichever first. June 28, then. She checks the tach against the last logbook entry. 3.0 hours since the effective date. Then the AD list in the engine logbook, where Marisol has already added it in pencil with the due column filled in. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Legal until June 28 or 47 more hours. She's due Tuesday anyway?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Tuesday. Because it's that valve, and because I've been wanting an excuse."</span> Jordan types it into the chat for Russ: <b>Not grounded. AD compliance by 6/28 or 50 hrs. Marisol's doing it Tuesday.</b>`,
          why:`ADs are mandatory (39.7), and their status must be recorded (91.417(a)(2)(v)). Checking applicability, compliance time, hours since the effective date, and the AD record shows whether the airplane is legal today. The PIC makes that call under 91.7(b).`},
        {t:`Fly, and treat the AD like factory advice for Marisol to handle.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Marisol puts her coffee down. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Factory advice is a service bulletin. This one's from the FAA. Part 39 calls it a rule, and flying past it is a violation."</span> She slides the printout across the bench. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Read the part that says when."</span>`,
          why:`Airworthiness directives are legally enforceable rules under Part 39, and operating an aircraft that doesn't comply with an applicable AD violates 39.7. Manufacturer service bulletins are generally optional for private operators. ADs aren't.`},
        {t:`Fly. ADs are for the owners to track, and she's flying the schedule.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"91.403 says owner or operator. You're both."</span> She points the coffee cup at her. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"And 91.7 says whoever's in the left seat decides if she's airworthy. That's also you, at nine."</span>`,
          why:`91.403(a) makes the owner or operator primarily responsible for AD compliance, and 91.7(b) makes the PIC responsible for determining airworthiness before flight. A pilot who's also an owner carries both.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s6`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.B`, title:`Show me she's legal`, q:`Playing examiner, Abby wants proof that Biscuit is airworthy and legal today. What should Jordan show?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`11:20 AM, after the lesson. 1.2 on the Hobbs, 0.7 of it under the hood, and her instrument line in the logbook finally said 3.1. Abby sat on an upturned bucket in the hangar with her coffee and her clipboard, and tried to look like a stranger, and failed.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I'm the examiner. We've just met. Show me this airplane is airworthy and legal to fly today."</span>`,`The squawk sheet was on the binder on the wing, the left fuel cap from December 22 written up and closed out. The maintenance logbooks were in Marisol's cabinet. The door post sleeve was right there, scratched and a little cloudy, the way it had been in every photo Danny ever sent.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The papers in the airplane, then the annual in the maintenance logbook.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Airworthiness certificate, registration, the annual signed in August. Abby writes something. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Good start. When was the transponder done? The ELT? What ADs apply, and are they current?"</span> Jordan goes back to the cabinet for the rest.`,
          why:`The documents aboard and the annual are central, but airworthiness also depends on the ELT, transponder, AD status, and open discrepancies (PA.I.B.K1). An examiner will ask about each.`},
        {t:`The club binder, with Marisol's word that it's all current.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Abby takes the binder and doesn't open it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Marisol's great. Marisol's not the pilot in command. 91.7 says you are. Find it for me."</span>`,
          why:`The mechanic signs the work, but the pilot in command determines whether the aircraft is in condition for safe flight. The applicant has to locate and explain the records herself (PA.I.B.S1, S2).`},
        {t:`The hangar whiteboard and the squawk sheet, with what's due.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Abby looks at the whiteboard, where ANNUAL 8/26 is still in Danny's capitals, five months stale. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You found the ELT battery in May because nobody was reading that board. The board's a copy. Show me the source."</span>`,
          why:`A due list is someone's copy of the records, and it can go stale. The legal record is the logbooks and the documents in the airplane.`},
        {t:`ARROW in the airplane, then the logbooks and the AD list.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Airworthiness certificate in the sleeve, readable from the door. Registration behind it. She reads the owner line before she can stop herself: HANGAR SIX FLYING CLUB LLC. Not four names. Not his. She'd looked up 47.41 at three in the morning once, the 30 days after the death of the holder, and never asked anybody. POH in the seat pocket, placards, the weight and balance in the back cover. Then the logbooks: annual August 20, 2026, M. Tran, IA, good through August 31, 2027. ELT inspected the same day, battery date on the unit from May. Transponder and static check October 7, good through October 2028. The AD list, with the selector line in pencil, due June 28. Squawk sheet, nothing open. Abby nods once. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, gentle, matter-of-fact.">"You were looking for his name."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quick, defensive, caught out.">"I was checking if she'd been illegal since April."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, gentle, matter-of-fact.">"Both. Fine. Both."</span>`,
          why:`PA.I.B.S1 and S2: locate the airworthiness and registration documents, and determine airworthiness from the inspection records, AD status, and discrepancies. ARROW covers what's aboard. The logbooks cover the rest.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s7`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`Change the number`, q:`Jordan's app scores are in the nineties, but Abby just caught a wrong number. How should Jordan study?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, January 6, two in the afternoon, the briefing room. Abby had her own logbook open next to Jordan's and was adding in the margin again. Thirteen hundred and thirty one. Her phone buzzed once face down on the table with a number from an area code Jordan didn't know, and Abby let it buzz.`,`Jordan had been scoring between 94 and 98 on the practice app for a week. She'd shown Abby the screenshots. Abby had looked at them the way she looked at a landing she hadn't seen.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"One question before I sign. A private pilot takes a third class medical at age 41. When does it run out for private privileges?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"End of the 60th month."</span> It came out before she'd thought about it. She heard it come out.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The app's question says 39. I said 41. You answered the app."</span> Six days to the test.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Run the app's whole question bank again, until she's seen every question twice.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You'll get faster at recognizing them. The FAA writes new ones. Then what?"</span> She asks two more with the numbers changed, and Jordan gets one.`,
          why:`Memorizing a question bank trains recognition, not understanding. When a number or a detail changes, a memorized answer becomes a wrong one.`},
        {t:`Take every miss back to the regulation and explain the reason out loud.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She builds a list on a legal pad, every miss from the app and from Abby's pre-solo test in October, and next to each one the section number. Then she explains each out loud to the whiteboard like it's a new EMT on their first shift. 61.23, 41 at the exam, 24th month. 830.2, hospitalized more than 48 hours. 91.417, AD status. Abby listens to six of them, stops her on one, makes her redo it, and then uncaps the green pen and signs. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Explain it like that on Tuesday and the computer won't know what hit it."</span>`,
          why:`Studying from the regulation, and explaining the reason out loud, builds the knowledge that survives a changed number. It's the same correlation the ACS asks for on the oral.`},
        {t:`Read Parts 61 and 91 straight through before Tuesday.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Thorough. Also about three hundred pages, and a lot of 61 is ATP and helicopter stuff you'll never see. Start with your misses and read outward from them."</span> Jordan starts with her misses.`,
          why:`Reading the source is better than memorizing answers, but reading all of it is unfocused for six days. Targeting the areas behind her misses, then the sections around them, uses the time better.`},
        {t:`Ease off for the week, with her app scores already in the nineties.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You just answered the wrong question with total confidence. That's not ready. That's fluent."</span> She leaves the pen capped for twenty minutes while Jordan works through her misses, and signs after.`,
          why:`A high practice score on memorized items can hide real gaps, as a changed number just showed. Confidence that hasn't been tested on new versions of a question isn't readiness.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s8`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.H`, title:`The night before`, q:`With the knowledge test tomorrow, how should Jordan spend tonight?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, January 11, 7:40 PM, her kitchen. The shift that ended at seven that morning had been a long one: a fall at a care home at three in the morning, an old man with a hip, and a daughter who held his hand the whole way in. Jordan had slept until half past one and felt mostly human.`,`The logbook was open on the table to the page with Abby's green ink.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, endorsements">01/06/27. I certify that Jordan E. Reyes has received the required training in accordance with 14 CFR § 61.105. I have determined they are prepared for the Private Pilot Airplane knowledge test.<br>/s/ 01/06/27 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027<br><br>01/06/27. I certify that Jordan E. Reyes has received the required training to qualify for solo flying. I have determined that they meet the applicable requirements of 14 CFR § 61.87(p) and are proficient to make solo flights in a Cessna 172N.<br>/s/ 01/06/27 A. Strand 3418862CFI RE 06-30-2027</prop>`,`On the back page, in her own handwriting, was the list she'd made in April and kept updating like a protocol card.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Jordan's logbook, back page: 61.109(a), as of 1/11/27">TOTAL ${(G.pushedweather)?`61.8`:`61.1`} (40) ✓. DUAL ${(G.pushedweather)?`53.9`:`53.2`} (20) ✓. SOLO 7.9 (10). 2.1 TO GO.<br>XC DUAL 4.4 (3) ✓.<br>NIGHT ${(G.pushedweather)?`6.3`:`5.6`} (3) ✓. NIGHT XC KAPA-KPUB-KAPA 160 NM 12/16 ✓. NIGHT FULL STOPS ${(G.pushedweather)?`13`:`12`} (10) ✓.<br>HOOD 3.1 (3) ✓.<br>SOLO XC 5.1 (5) ✓.<br>LONG SOLO XC 12/22: KAPA-KLIC-KPUB-KAPA, 207 NM, 3 FULL STOPS, KAPA-KLIC 57.7 (150 / 3 PTS / ONE OVER 50) ✓.<br>SOLO TOWERED: 3 T/O, 3 FULL STOP, KAPA, 10/20 ✓.<br>CHECKRIDE PREP 3.0 WITHIN 2 CAL MONTHS: 0.0.<br>KNOWLEDGE TEST: 1/12.</prop>`,`At 7:52 her mother called.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Is it the big test tomorrow?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"The written. The big one's after."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Quiet, tender, a little lost.">"Do you need anything?"</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Quiet, tender, a little lost.">"I don't know what you'd need."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take one more full sixty question practice test at ten tonight, timed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She finishes at 11:58, scores a 91, and lies awake until two arguing with herself about question 38. In the morning she packs in a hurry and makes it with eleven minutes to spare.`,
          why:`A late timed test the night before, on a post-shift day, costs sleep and adds anxiety without adding knowledge. IMSAFE applies to test day too: fatigue and stress both hurt performance.`},
        {t:`Review her weak list for twenty minutes, then pack and get to bed early.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Nothing, Ma. Sleep."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Then go sleep."</span> Twenty minutes on NTSB 830 and the chart legend. Then the bag: driver's license, current address. Logbook. The FTN on a sticky note. Her nine dollar plotter. The calculator. Two E6Bs, Danny's aluminum one with the pencil dot, and a plastic one from the pilot shop, clean, because Abby had texted in December, <b>Proctors look at E6Bs. Bring one with nothing on it.</b> Lights out at 9:50.`,
          why:`A short review of weak areas, the required documents packed, and a full night's sleep set up test day the way a good preflight sets up a flight. Rested beats crammed.`},
        {t:`Skip the books and the bag both, and have dinner at Claire's.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`Dinner is good and she sleeps well. In the morning she packs at 7:30 with a piece of toast in her mouth, and is halfway to the car before she goes back for the logbook with the endorsement in it.`,
          why:`Rest is the right instinct. But leaving the packing and the document check for the morning invites the scramble that leaves something behind, like the endorsement she can't test without.`},
        {t:`Stay up with the regulations tonight until she feels sure of them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`At 1 AM she's rereading 830.2 for the fourth time and can't remember what she read the third time. She goes to bed. The alarm is cruel.`,
          why:`Working on little sleep is a job skill, not a performance strategy. The handbooks treat fatigue as a hazard for exactly this reason. It's why Abby's rule is no solo the morning after a 24.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s9`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`The pencil dot`, q:`Danny's E6B has his pencil dot on the wind side. How should Jordan handle the testing room rules?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, January 12, 8:22 AM. The testing center was on the second floor of a tan office building off Arapahoe Road, between a dentist and an insurance agent, with a small PSI sign on the door and a waiting room with four chairs and a plant that might have been plastic.`,`The proctor had reading glasses on a beaded chain, a cardigan with a pen clipped to the pocket, and a second pen behind her ear for backup, which Jordan respected. She looked at the driver's license, then at Jordan, then at the license for slightly too long. <span class="said" data-who="Proctor">"You cut your hair."</span> Jordan had, in October. The DMV photo was from 2022 and had more hair in it and less of everything else. Logbook next, turned around on the counter so Abby's green ink could be read upside down, and then a photo against a white wall that Jordan figured would follow her to her grave. Phone, keys, coat, flight bag, locker 6. Locker 6 stuck. You had to hip it.`,`The aids went on the counter. The plotter got shoved right back. <span class="said" data-who="Proctor">"Clear that for me,"</span> the proctor said about the calculator, and Jordan did, and they both watched the little screen go to zero the way you'd watch a monitor. The plastic E6B got flipped twice and pronounced boring. Danny's weighed about four times as much, so of course it got the lamp. The proctor tipped the wind side back and forth under it until the light found the dot. Gray, smaller than a lentil, with a circle around it in the same pencil.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Proctor">"Is this mark yours?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quiet, plain, caught off guard.">"My brother's. From a flight."</span> Which flight, she had no idea. She'd never asked anybody, and now there was nobody to ask, which was a thought she hadn't planned on having at a counter in an office park at 8:24 in the morning.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Proctor">"Can't let anything written go in, hon. Erase it or it stays in the locker. Up to you."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask the proctor for an exception for one pencil dot.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The proctor lets her tell the whole thing, Danny and the dot and the eleven years, and Jordan hears herself telling it and can't find the brakes. When she's done the proctor says she's sorry, and means it, and slides the E6B back across the counter to her anyway. Locker 6, hip. She walks into the testing room four minutes behind her own schedule with her face hot and half her head still out in the hallway.`,
          why:`The rules on aids are applied the same way to everyone, and no written material may be taken into the testing area. Arguing with the proctor costs time and focus and won't change the answer.`},
        {t:`Leave Danny's in the locker and take the plastic one in.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quiet, sure, settled.">"It stays in the locker."</span> She'd known she would say that since December, when Abby texted about proctors, and she'd bought the plastic one the next morning for twenty dollars from the same guy who'd tried to upsell her on the plotter. Danny's goes back in locker 6, on his notebook, dot up. Hip. Three sheets of scratch paper, counted out loud by the proctor, who says she'll count them back in, and the supplement, which has furry corners and somebody's coffee ring on the cover that predates them both.`,
          why:`She planned for this by bringing a clean flight computer, which meets the rules on aids without touching the one that matters to her. Preparation turned a hard choice into an easy one.`},
        {t:`Rub the dot out with her thumb, and take his computer in.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She licks her thumb and rubs before she can talk herself out of it. Eleven years of pencil, gone in three passes: a gray smear, then a spot on the aluminum a little shinier than the rest. The proctor takes a look, nods, hands it back. It works fine. In two hours she never turns it over to the wind side, not even on the wind question, which she does on scratch paper instead with her mouth shut tight.`,
          why:`Erasing the mark satisfies the rule, so this is allowed. But she'd brought a clean computer for exactly this case, and the choice cost her something she didn't have to give up.`},
        {t:`Agree to the locker, then slip Danny's into her jacket.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`She says okay. Then she reaches, and the proctor, who has seen this exact move from people with much better poker faces, slides the computer back toward her own side of the counter and says, perfectly pleasant, <span class="said" data-who="Proctor">"Locker, or no test."</span> Locker. Jordan hasn't felt this fifteen since an assistant principal intercepted a note.`,
          why:`Taking unauthorized material into the testing area is cheating, which can void the test and bring FAA action. The center monitors applicants for exactly this reason.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s10`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.E`, title:`Figure 20-something`, q:`What's the best test strategy for a question that's eating the clock?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`9:19 AM. Question 14 of 60.`,`Question 9 had put a chart right on the screen, crisp and new, a piece of sectional with a Class D ring and a tower frequency box she was careful not to read. Question 14 sent her to the book. The supplement was spiral bound and soft at the corners from a few thousand nervous hands, and the figure was a sectional excerpt from somewhere in the middle of the country, printed in 2018 at what felt like half the size of the real thing.`,`What is the floor of controlled airspace over the private airport northeast of the city? She found the city. She found two airports. Neither had a name she could read without squinting. The clock in the corner of the screen said she'd been on this one for four minutes.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep hunting the figure until she finds the airport.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Nine more minutes. She finds it, finally, and gets it, probably, and then realizes she's thirteen minutes into one question and has forty six to go. She spends the next ten questions rushing.`,
          why:`At about two minutes a question, sinking thirteen into one steals time from the rest. Mark it and come back with fresh eyes.`},
        {t:`Pick her likeliest answer and flag it for review.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She narrows it to two, picks the one that matches the shading she can see, taps the flag, and goes to 15. By 25 she's back under two minutes a question and breathing normally.`,
          why:`An answered, flagged question can be revisited and still counts if time runs out. Moving on protects the time budget, and coming back later often makes the figure easier.`},
        {t:`Ask the proctor to point out the airport on the figure.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`The proctor comes over, looks at the screen, and says quietly, <span class="said" data-who="Proctor">"I can help with the equipment. I can't help with the questions."</span> Jordan knew that. She'd read it.`,
          why:`Proctors can explain how to use the testing equipment, not the content of questions. Asking costs time and gets nothing.`},
        {t:`Leave it blank, keep going, and come back to it at the end.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She skips it and keeps going, and the blank bothers her for the next twenty questions like a stone in a shoe. When she comes back she gets it in a minute.`,
          why:`Coming back later is right, but an unanswered question is wrong if time runs out. Choosing the best guess and flagging it gives the same chance to return without the risk.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s11`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`Forty minutes`, q:`With forty minutes left and everything answered, how should Jordan use the time?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`10:20 AM. Sixty of sixty answered. The clock said forty minutes left, and the review screen showed seven flags.`,`She sat back. Her shoulders were up around her ears, the way they got after a long call, and she put them down on purpose. Somewhere outside the room a printer was going and a man was asking the front desk about parking validation.`,`The cost-sharing question had come up as number 33, three people and a gas bill, and she'd done it the way she'd done it on the back of Claire's envelope. The NTSB one had been 51, a passenger with a broken bone, and she hadn't flagged it. She'd been sure.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Submit now, with every question answered and her first answers standing.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Her finger is on the button. She moves it off and goes back to the first flag, because Abby would ask, and because forty minutes is forty minutes.`,
          why:`Submitting with time left is allowed, and a well-prepared applicant may do fine. But the flagged questions were flagged for a reason, and the time is already paid for.`},
        {t:`Review the seven flags and change one only with a reason she can name.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She goes through them one at a time. Question 14, the tiny chart: with the book turned sideways under the lamp she finds the airport name this time and the shading, and changes her answer, because now she can say why. The other six she reads, works again, and leaves alone. At 10:58 she presses the button.`,
          why:`Reviewing flagged items with fresh eyes catches real errors. Changing an answer only when there's a specific reason avoids trading right answers for wrong ones out of nerves.`},
        {t:`Start at question one and work all sixty again with fresh eyes.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She gets to 38 before the clock says nine minutes. She's changed four answers and couldn't tell you why for three of them. She leaves the flags she never got to.`,
          why:`Redoing the whole test uses up the time meant for the flagged questions and invites changes without reasons.`},
        {t:`Go back through all sixty and change any answer that feels wrong now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Three of them feel wrong. She changes them. On the drive home she works one of the three again in her head and realizes she'd had it right the first time.`,
          why:`A feeling isn't new information. Changes should come from a specific reason, like a misread word or a figure checked again.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s12`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`The embossed seal`, q:`Jordan's knowledge test report just came off the press with its seal. What should she do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The screen went white for a second, which was the longest second of the year so far. Then a box. A score. A word.`,`<b>95. PASS.</b>`,`The proctor printed it at the front desk, ran it through a little hand press that clunked, and handed it over with the seal still warm, pressed into the middle right of the report, where you could feel it with your thumb.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Airman Knowledge Test Report"><code>FEDERAL AVIATION ADMINISTRATION<br>AIRMAN KNOWLEDGE TEST REPORT<br><br>NAME: REYES, JORDAN E<br>FTN: C2611804<br>EXAM: PRIVATE PILOT AIRPLANE (PAR)<br>EXAM DATE: 01/12/2027&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;TAKE: 1<br>SCORE: 95%&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;GRADE: PASS&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[SEAL]<br>EXPIRATION DATE: 01/31/2029<br><br>ACS CODES FOR INCORRECTLY ANSWERED QUESTIONS:<br>PA.I.D.K6<br>PA.I.E.K2<br>PA.III.A.K8<br><br>DO NOT LOSE THIS REPORT</code></prop>`,`Three codes. Three questions out of sixty. She stood in the hallway outside the suite by the elevator, with her coat on and the flight bag back on her shoulder, holding the paper by the edges.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fold it into her coat pocket, and text everyone the score from the car.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She texts four people before the car's warm. That evening she finds the report in the coat pocket, folded in quarters, with a coffee drip on the seal. She flattens it under a cookbook and writes the codes down before she forgets which pocket.`,
          why:`The original AKTR must be presented at the practical test, and its codes are the study plan. Treating it like a receipt risks losing both.`},
        {t:`Sleeve the original in her logbook, and look up the three codes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Logbook out of the bag, report into the clear sleeve inside the back cover, two photos. Then, in the car with the engine running, the ACS on her phone. PA.I.D.K6: inflight intercept procedures. PA.I.E.K2: chart symbols. PA.III.A.K8: NTSB accident and incident reporting. She reads the third one twice, and her face does something.`,
          why:`The AKTR is needed for the checkride, and the ACS codes identify the elements to restudy before the instructor's A.2 endorsement and the examiner's oral. Looking them up right away turns a score into a plan.`},
        {t:`Snap a picture, and toss the paper in the bag. PSI can print another.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She's right, as far as it goes. The FAA's FAQ says PSI can print a duplicate for any test on or after January 13, 2020. That night she digs the paper back out from under a granola bar anyway, flattens it, and slides it into her logbook, because the copy with the embossed seal is the one she wants to hand an examiner.`,
          why:`A duplicate AKTR can be printed from PSI for tests taken on or after January 13, 2020, so this isn't a disaster. Keeping the original safe is still the simplest way to be ready for the practical test.`},
        {t:`Put it in the glovebox, and deal with the codes another day.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`It stays in the glovebox for three days, under the registration and a tire gauge, until Abby texts <b>Codes?</b> and Jordan has to go out to the parking lot in her socks.`,
          why:`Passing gets the AKTR, but the missed codes drive the required instructor review and the checkride oral. Putting them off wastes the best time to study them.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f11s3`, title:`Pro rata, on the back of an envelope`,
     setup:[`Say it's next December and Jordan, a private pilot now, flies Claire and Ellie to Pueblo for her mother's birthday. Afterward Biscuit takes 17.5 gallons to refill at $6.40 a gallon, plus one quart of oil at $11.00. Pueblo charges a $10.00 ramp fee.`,`Her share of the club's monthly payment that month is $410, which covers insurance, the hangar, and the maintenance reserve.`,`61.113(c): the pilot may not pay less than the pro rata share of fuel, oil, airport expenditures, or rental fees. Pro rata means divided evenly among everyone aboard, pilot included. Round to the nearest cent, and never round the pilot's share down.`],
     fields:[{label:`Shareable expenses for the trip ($)`,answer:133,tol:0.01},{label:`Least Jordan may pay with three aboard ($)`,answer:44.34,tol:0.005},{label:`Most Claire may pay for herself and Ellie ($)`,answer:88.66,tol:0.005},{label:`Least Jordan may pay if Ellie stays home ($)`,answer:66.5,tol:0.01},{label:`Part of the $410 that Claire may share ($)`,answer:0,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Fuel: 17.5 × 6.40 = $112.00. Oil: $11.00. Ramp fee: $10.00. Shareable total: 112.00 + 11.00 + 10.00 = $133.00.`,`Three aboard: 133.00 ÷ 3 = $44.333. Round up to $44.34, because $44.33 would be a fraction of a cent under her share. That's the least Jordan may pay. She can pay more.`,`Claire can cover the rest: 133.00 minus 44.34 = $88.66 for the two of them. Ellie counts as a person.`,`Two aboard: 133.00 ÷ 2 = $66.50.`,`The $410 is insurance, hangar, and maintenance. None of those are on the list in 61.113(c), so none of it is shareable. Zero.`]},
    {after:`f11s4`, title:`Calendar months`,
     setup:[`Every clock in this chapter runs to the end of a month. Enter months as numbers (January is 1) and years as four digits.`,`Russ's flight review: December 12, 2024. 61.56(c): since the beginning of the 24th calendar month before the month of the flight. Russ moved on November 20. 61.60: 30 days.`,`Jordan's third class medical: April 2026, age 35. Walt's: March 2025, age over 40. 61.23(d) for private privileges: 60th month under 40, 24th month at 40 or older.`,`Jordan's knowledge test: January 12, 2027. 61.39(a)(1): within the 24 calendar months before the month the practical test is completed. Her checkride prep must be within the 2 calendar months before the month of the test.`],
     fields:[{label:`Last month Russ may act as PIC on the 2024 review (month)`,answer:12,tol:0},{label:`...and its year`,answer:2026,tol:0},{label:`Days past the 61.60 limit on December 28`,answer:8,tol:0},{label:`Year Jordan's medical expires`,answer:2031,tol:0},{label:`Month Walt's medical expires`,answer:3,tol:0},{label:`Year Walt's medical expires`,answer:2027,tol:0},{label:`Year Jordan's AKTR stops covering a checkride`,answer:2029,tol:0},{label:`For a March checkride, earliest month her prep counts`,answer:1,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Russ: flying in December 2026, the 24th calendar month before is December 2024, so the December 12 review counts through December 31, 2026. In January 2027 the window starts January 2025, and the review falls outside it. Month 12, year 2026.`,`Address: November 20 plus 30 days is December 20. December 28 is 8 days past. He hadn't flown since, so he just needs to notify before he does.`,`Jordan's medical: under 40, 60th month after April 2026 is April 2031. Walt's: 40 or older, 24th month after March 2025 is March 2027, through March 31.`,`AKTR: passed in January 2027, so the practical test must be completed by January 31, 2029.`,`Prep for a March 2027 checkride: the 2 calendar months before March are January and February. December doesn't count. Month 1.`]},
    {after:`f11s12`, title:`The numbers on the screen`,
     setup:[`The PAR test: 60 scored questions, 2.0 hours, 70 percent to pass. Jordan answered 57 correctly. Danny, eleven years ago, answered 54 of his 60 correctly. Jordan finished her first pass through all sixty after 80 minutes.`],
     fields:[{label:`Correct answers needed to pass`,answer:42,tol:0},{label:`Questions you can miss and still pass`,answer:18,tol:0},{label:`Jordan's score (percent)`,answer:95,tol:0},{label:`Danny's score (percent)`,answer:90,tol:0},{label:`Average minutes allowed per question`,answer:2,tol:0},{label:`Minutes left for review after her first pass`,answer:40,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Passing: 60 × 0.70 = 42 correct. 60 minus 42 = 18 misses allowed.`,`Jordan: 57 ÷ 60 = 0.95, so 95. Danny: 54 ÷ 60 = 0.90, so 90.`,`Time: 2.0 hours is 120 minutes. 120 ÷ 60 = 2 minutes a question. 120 minus 80 = 40 minutes left.`,`One more number, for the record: with sixty questions, scores move in steps of 1 2/3 points. 57 right is 95, and 58 right is 96.67, which the report would show as 97. Nobody gets a 96.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f11s6`, title:`Which regulation answers this?`, intro:`Walt ran the January safety meeting with an index card for each scenario and the pastries Russ had brought back from Pueblo on the table, one missing. He'd read the card and point at somebody. Pick the section that settles it.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Claire wants to pay for all the gas on the Akron trip.`, choices:[`61.113(c)`,`61.57(a)`,`91.7(b)`], answer:0, why:`61.113(c): the pilot pays at least her pro rata share of fuel, oil, airport expenditures, or rental fees.`},
      {prompt:`Russ moved to Lone Tree in November and hasn't told the FAA.`, choices:[`61.56(c)`,`61.60`,`61.3(a)`], answer:1, why:`61.60: notify the Airman Certification Branch in writing within 30 days of a change of permanent mailing address, or stop exercising privileges.`},
      {prompt:`Russ's last flight review was in December 2024. It's New Year's Day.`, choices:[`61.57(a)`,`61.23(d)`,`61.56(c)`], answer:2, why:`61.56(c): a flight review since the beginning of the 24th calendar month before the month of the flight.`},
      {prompt:`After her checkride, Jordan wants to fly Ellie home from Pueblo after dinner, at night.`, choices:[`61.57(b)`,`61.87(o)`,`91.151(a)(1)`], answer:0, why:`61.57(b): three takeoffs and three full-stop landings between 1 hour after sunset and 1 hour before sunrise, within 90 days, to carry passengers in that period.`},
      {prompt:`Walt's third class medical was issued in March 2025. When does it run out?`, choices:[`61.53`,`61.23(d)`,`61.39(a)(4)`], answer:1, why:`61.23(d): for private privileges at 40 or older at the exam, the end of the 24th month. 61.53 is about flying with a disqualifying condition or medication.`},
      {prompt:`What has to be in Biscuit before she moves.`, choices:[`91.203 and 91.9`,`91.417(a)`,`91.409(a)`], answer:0, why:`91.203: airworthiness certificate displayed and registration aboard. 91.9: the flight manual, markings, and placards. The maintenance records in 91.417 stay on the ground.`},
      {prompt:`Who decides whether Biscuit is in condition for safe flight today.`, choices:[`91.403(a)`,`91.7(b)`,`91.3(b)`], answer:1, why:`91.7(b): the pilot in command determines whether the aircraft is in condition for safe flight. 91.403(a) is the owner's maintenance responsibility.`},
      {prompt:`The ELT has transmitted for 70 minutes in total since its battery went in.`, choices:[`91.207(c)`,`91.207(d)`,`91.413(a)`], answer:0, why:`91.207(c)(1): replace the battery when the transmitter has been in use for more than 1 cumulative hour.`},
      {prompt:`Biscuit's annual was signed on August 20. How long is it good?`, choices:[`91.409(b)`,`91.409(a)`,`91.417(b)`], answer:1, why:`91.409(a): an annual within the preceding 12 calendar months, so this one runs through August 31 next year.`},
      {prompt:`Where the selector AD's status has to be written down.`, choices:[`39.7`,`91.403(a)`,`91.417(a)(2)(v)`], answer:2, why:`91.417(a)(2)(v): current status of applicable ADs, method of compliance, and the next due time for recurring ones.`},
      {prompt:`A passenger breaks a wrist in a hard landing.`, choices:[`49 CFR 830.2 and 830.5`,`49 CFR 830.15 only`,`14 CFR 91.25`], answer:0, why:`A fracture other than a simple finger, toe, or nose fracture is a serious injury under 830.2, which makes it an accident, and 830.5 requires immediate notification.`},
      {prompt:`When the written accident report is due.`, choices:[`830.10`,`830.15`,`830.6`], answer:1, why:`830.15(a): Form 6120.1/2 within 10 days after an accident, or after 7 days if an overdue aircraft is still missing.`},
      {prompt:`Jordan passed in January 2027. How late can her checkride be?`, choices:[`61.39(a)(1)`,`61.49(a)`,`61.35(a)(3)`], answer:0, why:`61.39(a)(1): the knowledge test must be passed within the 24 calendar months before the month the practical test is completed.`},
      {prompt:`Someone shows up at the testing center with no endorsement.`, choices:[`61.39(a)(6)`,`61.35(a)(1)`,`61.56(g)`], answer:1, why:`61.35(a)(1): an applicant for a knowledge test needs an instructor's endorsement, if this part requires one, for the ground training or home study. 61.103(d) requires it for the private.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`A private pilot flies two friends to a destination the pilot chose, for the pilot's own reasons. Fuel, oil, and airport fees total $240. The least the pilot may pay is`, opts:[`$60`,`$80`,`$120`], a:1, why:`61.113(c): pro rata means divided among everyone aboard, the pilot included. $240 ÷ 3 = $80. The pilot may pay more, never less.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Which flight meets the FAA's common purpose test for sharing expenses?`, opts:[`A friend asks to be flown to pick up a car and will pay for gas`,`The pilot advertises open seats on a public website`,`The pilot flies to a family dinner with a relative who's also going`], a:2, why:`AC 61-142: the pilot must have her own reason to go and choose the destination. Flying only because a passenger asked lacks a common purpose, and advertising to the public is holding out.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A private pilot's flight review was completed on June 9, 2025. With no other qualifying event, the pilot may act as PIC through`, opts:[`June 30, 2027`,`June 9, 2027`,`July 31, 2027`], a:0, why:`61.56(c) counts from the beginning of the 24th calendar month before the month of the flight, so a review any day in June 2025 is good through the end of June 2027.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`After changing permanent mailing addresses, a pilot may not exercise certificate privileges after how many days unless the FAA has been notified in writing?`, opts:[`10 days`,`30 days`,`60 days`], a:1, why:`61.60: 30 days, notifying the FAA Airman Certification Branch in writing.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A pilot was 38 years old when they received a first class medical. For private pilot privileges only, it expires at the end of the`, opts:[`12th month after the exam month`,`24th month after the exam month`,`60th month after the exam month`], a:2, why:`61.23(d): a first class medical used for private privileges by someone under 40 at the exam lasts through the 60th month. The 12 months apply to ATP pilot in command privileges.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An applicant passed the private pilot knowledge test on May 18, 2026. To use that result, the practical test must be completed by`, opts:[`May 31, 2028`,`May 18, 2028`,`May 31, 2027`], a:0, why:`61.39(a)(1): within the 24 calendar months preceding the month the practical test is completed. Passed in May 2026, good through the end of May 2028.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Which of these must be aboard the airplane during flight?`, opts:[`The engine and airframe logbooks`,`The registration certificate`,`The pilot's logbook, on any flight`], a:1, why:`91.203(a) requires the airworthiness certificate and registration aboard. Maintenance records are kept by the owner, and a student carries the logbook on solo cross-countries.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Who is primarily responsible for maintaining an aircraft in an airworthy condition, including compliance with airworthiness directives?`, opts:[`The owner or operator of the aircraft`,`The mechanic who last signed the logbook`,`The pilot who last flew the aircraft`], a:0, why:`91.403(a): the owner or operator is primarily responsible, including Part 39 compliance. The PIC still determines airworthiness before each flight under 91.7(b).`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An airworthiness directive applies to an aircraft. Compliance is`, opts:[`mandatory, within the AD's compliance time`,`optional unless the aircraft is flown for hire`,`required only at the next annual inspection`], a:0, why:`ADs are legally enforceable rules (39.3), and operating a product that doesn't meet an applicable AD is a violation (39.7). Each AD sets its own compliance time.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`During a postflight inspection, a pilot finds a cracked fuel cap gasket and fuel stains on the wing. The pilot should`, opts:[`mention it to the next pilot only if asked about it`,`note it and fly the next leg with extra fuel`,`document it and notify maintenance and the next pilot`], a:2, why:`PA.XII.A.S4: conduct a postflight inspection and document discrepancies and servicing requirements. A leaking cap needs a mechanic before the next flight.`, task:`XII`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Under 49 CFR 830.2, which of these is a serious injury?`, opts:[`A simple fracture of a finger`,`A fractured wrist`,`A sprained ankle`], a:1, why:`Serious injury includes any bone fracture except simple fractures of fingers, toes, or nose, plus hospitalization over 48 hours, severe hemorrhage, internal organs, and significant burns.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`After an aircraft accident, the operator must file the NTSB report within`, opts:[`7 days`,`30 days`,`10 days`], a:2, why:`830.15(a): within 10 days after an accident. The 7 days applies to an overdue aircraft that is still missing.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, airworthiness. A Special Airworthiness Information Bulletin (SAIB) is`, opts:[`a mandatory fix that must be done by its compliance time`,`advice about an airworthiness concern, not a requirement`,`a permit to ferry an unairworthy airplane to a repair shop`], a:1, why:`An SAIB alerts owners to an airworthiness concern that doesn't rise to an AD and makes recommendations; compliance isn't required. An AD is mandatory, and a special flight permit is what lets an airplane that doesn't meet its airworthiness requirements fly to a shop.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Spaced review, navigation. After flying 30 NM, a pilot finds the airplane 3 NM left of course. By the 60 to 1 rule, the airplane is off course by about`, opts:[`6 degrees`,`3 degrees`,`9 degrees`], a:0, why:`60 to 1 rule: degrees off course = distance off ÷ distance flown × 60 = 3 ÷ 30 × 60 = 6 degrees.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`Spaced review, emergencies. An airplane with a 9 to 1 glide ratio loses its engine at 3,000 feet AGL. In still air it can glide about`, opts:[`2.2 NM`,`6.6 NM`,`4.4 NM`], a:2, why:`3,000 × 9 = 27,000 feet. 27,000 ÷ 6,076 feet per nautical mile ≈ 4.4 NM. Wind changes the distance over the ground.`, task:`IX`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {q:`Over a congested area, 14 CFR 91.119 requires an airplane to fly at least`, opts:[`500 feet above the highest obstacle within 1,000 feet`,`1,000 feet above the highest obstacle within 2,000 feet`,`1,000 feet above the ground, whatever the obstacles`], a:1, why:`91.119(b): over a congested area, at least 1,000 feet above the highest obstacle within a horizontal radius of 2,000 feet of the aircraft, except for takeoff and landing.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Over open water or a sparsely populated area, 91.119 requires an airplane to stay at least`, opts:[`500 feet from any person, vessel, vehicle, or structure`,`1,000 feet above the surface at all times, day or night`,`2,000 feet horizontally from any boat, road, or building`], a:0, why:`91.119(c): over areas other than congested areas, 500 feet above the surface, except over open water or sparsely populated areas, where the aircraft may not be closer than 500 feet to any person, vessel, vehicle, or structure.`, task:`I.D`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Before acting as pilot in command of an airplane with a 230 horsepower engine, a private pilot needs`, opts:[`a high performance class rating added by a practical test`,`a one time high performance endorsement from an instructor`,`at least 10 hours as pilot in command in that make and model`], a:1, why:`61.31(f): a high performance airplane has an engine of more than 200 horsepower, and acting as PIC requires ground and flight training and a one time logbook endorsement from an authorized instructor. It isn't a rating, and there's no hour minimum.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Under 14 CFR 61.31, a complex airplane is one with`, opts:[`an engine of more than 200 horsepower and electric flaps`,`a pressurized cabin and a service ceiling above 25,000 feet`,`retractable gear, flaps, and a controllable pitch propeller`], a:2, why:`61.31(e): a complex airplane has retractable landing gear, flaps, and a controllable pitch propeller (or FADEC). More than 200 horsepower is high performance, and pressurized above 25,000 feet is the high altitude endorsement.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A private pilot is convicted of driving while intoxicated. Under 14 CFR 61.15, the pilot must send the FAA a written report within`, opts:[`60 days of the motor vehicle action`,`30 days of the motor vehicle action`,`10 days of the motor vehicle action`], a:0, why:`61.15(e): each person holding a certificate must give the FAA a written report of each motor vehicle action within 60 days. Two motor vehicle actions within 3 years are grounds to deny, suspend, or revoke a certificate.`, task:`I.H`, domain:`Preflight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{
    const p=(s.picks||{});
    const rubbed=(p[8]===2);
    return [
    `In the car, with the engine running and the heater roaring on her feet, she sent one text. <b>95. Pass.</b> To Abby, first, because Abby had asked for the date before Jordan clicked anything and had earned the number before anybody else.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="11:12 AM">${(s.score>=27)?`Of course. Send me the codes. All of them. Then go eat something that isn't from a vending machine.`:`Good. Now send me the codes, and we'll talk about the whole stretch from December, not only the score. Eat something first.`}</prop>`,
    `She sent the codes. Then she sent the score to Walt, because he'd asked to see her logbook every month since April and this was going in it. Walt answered in under a minute, which meant he'd had the phone in his hand.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Walt Herrera" time="11:15 AM">9.</prop>`,
    `Then, a minute later: <b>Nobody gets a ten.</b>`,
    `She laughed out loud in the parking lot, alone, with the windows fogging up, and a woman walking to the dentist's office looked over at her and then away.`,
    `Her mother called at 11:20. First ring on Jordan's end, which was a reversal of the usual order.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes">"Well?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Ninety five, Ma. I passed."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Measured, considering, a little wistful.">"Ninety five."</span> She could hear her mother deciding whether that was good. <span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Measured, considering, a little wistful.">"Your brother got a hundred on his. He called me from the parking lot."</span>`,
    `Jordan opened her mouth and closed it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lourdes" data-tone="Warm, quick, mothering.">"But ninety five is very good, mija. Very good. Are you eating?"</span>`,
    `She said she was going to. When her mother hung up she sat with the phone in her lap for a while. Then she reached into the back seat for the flight bag and took out the green notebook. His, after hers. She'd found the page in December, an August from eleven years ago, a month after his first solo cross-country. She'd folded the corner down without reading it and kept the rule, even on the nights it was hard. It wasn't hard today. It was strange.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from August, eleven years ago">Aug 27. No flying. THE WRITTEN.<br>90.<br>Sixty questions in 41 minutes because I wanted lunch. Lady at the desk said most people use the whole two and a half hours. I said most people aren't me. She didn't laugh. Fair.<br>Missed six. The report lists them as codes, PLT this and PLT that, like license plates. Looked them up in the truck. Three on the sectional, the little print, I'm not old, it's small. One on the NTSB, where apparently a broken finger isn't serious, tell that to my finger. Two on whether my passengers can pay for the gas. They can't. Not all of it. Write it on your hand, Danny.<br>Instructor says 90's great. Instructor says that to everyone.<br>Called Ma, told her 100. She'll never check. Don't tell Mom.<br>Jordan would get a 96 and be mad about the 4.</prop>`,
    `She read it twice. Then she laughed again, a different laugh, the one that comes out when nothing's funny.`,
    `There's no such thing as a 96, she wanted to tell him. Sixty questions don't make a 96. They go 95, then 97. He'd have argued. He'd have said that's not the point, Jordo, the point is you'd be mad about it, and she'd have said the point is you can't do math, and Claire would have told them both to stop it in front of the baby.`,
    `He'd have been wrong about the number and right about the rest. She was mad about the five. She'd been mad about it since the screen went white.`,
    `And two of her three were his. The little print on the sectional. The NTSB. She knew which question it had been, the moment she read PA.III.A.K8 in the car: a passenger with a broken bone, and she'd answered it as a paramedic, because a fracture is a fracture when you're the one splinting it, and the regulation had a list of exceptions she'd read four times and then walked straight past. He'd missed it with a finger. She'd missed it with fifteen years of patients. Same question, eleven years apart, and neither of them had flagged it.`,
    `The one she'd gotten that he hadn't was the gas money. Twice, he'd missed it. She thought about Claire's envelope and Ellie's quarters, and that she'd learned it at his kitchen counter, with his daughter asking if she counted.`,
    `For thirty five years she had been the one with the better grades and he had been the one with the better stories, and the deal had always been that he got to tease her about the grades. She'd beaten him. Five points. And there was nobody to call about it except a woman who thought he'd gotten a hundred, and she wasn't going to tell her. Don't tell Mom. That was twice now. She could do it for him twice.`,
    `${rubbed?`His E6B was in the side pocket of the bag, clean on the wind side now, a little gray where the dot had been. She ran her thumb over the spot. It felt like nothing, the way it always would from now on, and she put it back.`:`His E6B was in the side pocket of the bag where she'd put it back after the locker, the pencil dot with its little circle still right where he'd left it on the wind side. She didn't look at it. She knew it was there.`}`,
    `She put the notebook back in the bag, next to her own logbook with the report in its sleeve. Two records of the same test, and both of them honest about the score. Only one of the two people who'd taken it had told Ma the truth.`,
    `At 2:40 that afternoon, Abby's study plan came in, in three parts, the way she sent everything.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="2:40 PM">PA.I.D.K6, intercepts. The intercept section of the AIM and the signals, both directions. You'll explain it to me Friday like I'm a fighter pilot who's annoyed with you.<br><br>PA.I.E.K2, chart symbols. The sectional legend. Every symbol on it, on my table, with a highlighter. Bring the reading glasses you pretend you don't need.<br><br>PA.III.A.K8, NTSB. 830.2, out loud, as a lawyer and not a medic. A simple finger fracture isn't serious. I know. I know. Then I sign A.2, and the examiner gets to ask you all three anyway. That's the whole oral, plus everything else.</prop>`,
    `That night at the kitchen table she opened her logbook to the back page and, under the last line of the 61.109 list, wrote the result in pencil, small, in her capitals, the way she'd write a set of vitals she'd need to hand off later.`,
    `<b>KNOWLEDGE TEST 1/12/27: 95. PASS. GOOD THROUGH 1/31/29.<br>TO FIX BEFORE THE ORAL: PA.I.D.K6 INTERCEPTS. PA.I.E.K2 CHART SYMBOLS. PA.III.A.K8 NTSB.</b>`
    ];
  },
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch11.mp3`, len:`about 60 minutes`},
  next:`Checkride`
});
